\chapter{Experiments}
\setcounter{section}{0}

This chapter presents the experimental work in the order in which it actually unfolded during the project, from the early hand-detection and letter-classification probes that motivated the methodological pivot, through the architecture comparison and hyperparameter sweep that produced the champion configuration, to the multi-seed and cross-dataset validation that closed the loop. Every number reported below is traceable to a file in \texttt{05\_experiments/}; the audit at \texttt{docs/chapter6\_data\_audit.md} maps each figure in this chapter to its source.

\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:exp_methodology}

The experimental design rests on two complementary protocols. The first is a \textbf{multi-seed protocol} that runs every headline configuration with three random seeds (42, 123, 2026) and reports metrics as mean $\pm$ standard deviation. The motivation is statistical: the WLASL30 test split contains only 34 samples, so a single misclassification moves Top-1 by approximately 2.94 percentage points. A single-seed Top-1 figure on this test set is therefore indistinguishable from initialisation noise, and any claim about the system's behaviour requires aggregating across seeds. The second protocol is \textbf{Stratified 5-Fold Cross-Validation} on WLASL100 filtered to the same 30 classes (332 total samples, 5 non-overlapping folds with class distribution preserved); it complements the multi-seed protocol by varying the test split itself rather than only the model initialisation.

Four metrics are reported throughout the chapter. \textbf{Top-1 accuracy} is the proportion of test samples for which the highest-scoring prediction matches the ground truth and is the headline figure most often quoted. \textbf{Top-5 accuracy} is the proportion of test samples for which the ground truth appears anywhere in the five highest-scoring predictions; it matters in this domain because a real-time captioning system can present a shortlist when the Top-1 confidence is low. \textbf{Macro-F1} is the unweighted mean of per-class F1, which exposes degradation on rare classes that Top-1 averages would hide. \textbf{Balanced accuracy} averages per-class recall, providing a complementary view of class-imbalance effects.

All training runs share the same hardware and optimisation schedule. Training and evaluation execute on a local workstation with an NVIDIA RTX 3050 Ti Laptop GPU (4\,GB VRAM, CUDA 12.x). The optimiser is AdamW with learning rate $1 \times 10^{-3}$, weight decay $5 \times 10^{-4}$, and label smoothing of $0.1$ on the cross-entropy loss. The learning rate follows a CosineAnnealingLR schedule. Early stopping monitors validation Top-1 with patience 30 epochs, and the maximum run length is 150 epochs. These values are fixed across every experiment in this chapter unless explicitly noted otherwise.

Test-Time Augmentation (TTA) was deliberately excluded from the evaluation protocol. Earlier exploratory work compared TTA-enabled and TTA-disabled runs on WLASL30 (see \texttt{ensemble\_with\_tta} versus \texttt{ensemble\_no\_tta} in \texttt{05\_experiments/final\_assault/final\_results.json}) and found that TTA inflates the reported Top-1 without producing a corresponding gain in true generalisation. The decision is methodological rather than incidental: every number in this chapter reflects a single forward pass per test sample.

\section{Phase 1: Exploratory Work and Methodological Pivot}
\label{sec:exp_phase1}

The first months of the project explored two avenues that were eventually superseded by the landmark + word approach. They are reported here because the lessons drawn from them are the technical justification of the pivot, not because they appear as headline numbers in the final system.

\paragraph{Hand detection with YOLOv8n.} An initial sub-system trained YOLOv8n \cite{jocher2023yolov8} on the joint EgoHands and Kaggle Hand Dataset corpus described in Section~\ref{subsec:datasets_hd} (approximately 5{,}223 annotated images after stratified train/val/test split and duplicate removal). The model reached approximately \textbf{98\,\% mAP@0.5} and a \textbf{Mean IoU of approximately 87\,\%} on the held-out test split (verified in \texttt{05\_experiments/hand\_bbox\_baseline/hands\_model\_comparison.csv}: $\mathrm{mAP50} = 0.9897$, $\mathrm{mean\_IoU} = 0.8817$ for the base learning-rate run, with a sister low-learning-rate run within $0.4$ pp on both metrics). YOLOv8n was selected after a wider comparison against SSDLite-MobileNetV3 and EfficientDet-D0; the YOLO family won on inference latency at comparable detection accuracy on this dataset.

\paragraph{Letter-level pixel classification.} A parallel track trained CNN classifiers on the union of Sign Language MNIST (DS-03) and ASL Alphabet (DS-04). Coverage was limited to 24 of the 26 ASL letters because \emph{J} and \emph{Z} require hand motion and cannot be represented by a single static image. Accuracy on this static-frame classification task was high, but the task was also far simpler than the eventual project goal: predicting one of $\sim$24 hand poses per frame is a different problem from predicting a word from a multi-frame sign.

\paragraph{What the exploration revealed.} The bounding-box detector was not the bottleneck, since it worked reliably. The bottleneck was \emph{representation}. Pixel-based recognition has three properties that are problematic at the WLASL scale: (i) it carries appearance signal (skin tone, lighting, background) that is irrelevant to the linguistic content of a sign and that the model has to learn to discount with limited data; (ii) the parameter cost of an image-conditioned sequence model grows quickly, which makes overfitting on a few hundred clips per class very likely; and (iii) the geometry of hand articulation, which is the actual carrier of meaning, is not made explicit to the model.

The pivot to landmark-based word recognition replaced the visual problem with a geometric one: each frame is reduced from a $224 \times 224$ RGB tensor to 21 three-dimensional keypoints (63 features), a reduction of approximately $2{,}389\times$ in input dimensionality. The representation discards background and appearance at the source and exposes the joint geometry directly to a temporal model. The remainder of this chapter studies the resulting pipeline.

\section{Architecture Selection on WLASL30}
\label{sec:exp_phase2}

WLASL30 (the 30 most frequent WLASL classes; 242 train / 56 validation / 34 test clips) was used as a controlled bench to compare candidate architectures under matched conditions. All five rows in Table~\ref{tab:exp_arch_comparison} share the same dataset, the same input pipeline, the same hardware, and the same hyperparameters from Section~\ref{sec:exp_methodology}, with the exception of the Transformer row whose configuration uses $d_{\mathrm{model}} = 256$, $\mathrm{nhead} = 8$, four transformer layers, $\mathrm{lr} = 5 \times 10^{-4}$, $\mathrm{wd} = 1 \times 10^{-4}$, and dropout $0.3$ (verified in \texttt{config\_transformer} of \texttt{final\_assault/final\_results.json}).

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\caption{Single-split test results of the five architectures evaluated on WLASL30 (34 test samples). Numbers come from \texttt{05\_experiments/final\_assault/final\_results.json}. Standard deviations are not available at this stage; the multi-seed validation of the champion is in Section~\ref{sec:exp_wlasl30}.}
\label{tab:exp_arch_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l r r p{4.6cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Architecture} & \textbf{Top-1 (\%)} & \textbf{Top-5 (\%)} & \textbf{Notes} \\
\midrule
LSTM (landmarks)         & 41.18 & 67.65 & Plain LSTM baseline. \\
GRU (landmarks)          & 38.24 & 76.47 & Plain bidirectional GRU. \\
GRU + Enriched features  & 47.06 & 79.41 & Bone vectors and joint-angle augmentations. \\
\textbf{GRU + Attention} & \textbf{58.82} & \textbf{91.18} & Champion; 2.26\,M parameters. \\
Transformer              & 50.00 & 73.53 & Four layers, 8 heads, $d_{\mathrm{model}} = 256$. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The ordering in Table~\ref{tab:exp_arch_comparison} is consistent with the data regime. Plain GRU and plain LSTM are both starved for training data at 242 examples spread over 30 classes; with a comparable parameter budget, the GRU's lower count reduces overfitting at the cost of some ceiling. Adding hand-engineered enriched features (bone vectors, inter-joint angles) gives the model a 9-point absolute Top-1 lift, but the gain disappears once the model is given a Temporal Attention layer that can learn the discriminative time slices on its own. The Attention head dominates because sign language clips contain a small number of \emph{key frames} where the linguistic content is concentrated, surrounded by neutral preparation and recovery frames; weighting the GRU outputs by a learned attention distribution focuses capacity where it matters and tolerates variation in execution speed across signers.

The Transformer row is the most pedagogically interesting. With a parameter budget that is competitive with the GRU+Attention model, the Transformer underperforms on Top-1 (50.00 vs.\ 58.82) and underperforms more sharply on Top-5 (73.53 vs.\ 91.18). The result is consistent with the observation in the literature that recurrent inductive biases are an asset in low-data regimes; the Transformer's lack of a built-in sequential prior would plausibly be amortised at WLASL2000 scale, but the experiment in this section does not provide that scale.

The champion configuration, a bidirectional GRU with hidden size 256, two layers, dropout $0.4$, followed by a Temporal Attention head, is therefore selected as the default for every downstream experiment in the chapter. Its confusion matrix on the WLASL30 test split is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:exp_cm_wlasl30}.

\image{results/confusion_matrix_wlasl30_attention.png}{0.85}{Row-normalised confusion matrix of the GRU+Attention champion on the WLASL30 test split (34 samples). Source: \texttt{05\_experiments/final\_assault/confusion\_matrix\_normalized\_true.png}}{fig:exp_cm_wlasl30}

The matrix exposes two structural failure modes. The first is class imbalance at evaluation time: several test classes have only one or two samples, so a single error is enough to drop their per-class recall to zero. The second is genuine visual similarity between specific signs. The most common confusions on this split are \emph{candy} $\rightarrow$ \emph{who} and \emph{what} $\rightarrow$ \emph{book}, both at 2 of 3 affected samples, plus single-sample failures on \emph{fine} $\rightarrow$ \emph{help}, \emph{thanksgiving} $\rightarrow$ \emph{black}, and \emph{cool} $\rightarrow$ \emph{computer} (verified in \texttt{final\_assault/top\_confusions.json}).

\section{Sequence Length Sweep}
\label{sec:exp_seqlen}

The maximum sequence length \texttt{max\_frames} is the only hyperparameter that the model sees as a structural input shape rather than as a regulariser, so its value has to be defended explicitly. Subsampling too aggressively (32 frames) discards temporal dynamics; oversampling (96 frames) introduces padding and noise on shorter clips. A multi-seed sweep distinguishes signal from initialisation variance.

The sweep covers nine runs: 3 seeds (42, 123, 2026) $\times$ 3 sequence lengths (32, 64, 96), with every other variable held fixed at the values in Section~\ref{sec:exp_methodology}. Aggregated results from \texttt{05\_experiments/sequence\_length\_sweep/sweep\_summary.md} are in Table~\ref{tab:exp_seqlen_sweep}.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\caption{Sequence-length sweep on WLASL30 with 3 seeds per row. Mean $\pm$ standard deviation across seeds. Top-1 standard deviations are bounded below by the test-set granularity ($\approx$2.94\,pp per misclassification on 34 samples). Source: \texttt{05\_experiments/sequence\_length\_sweep/sweep\_summary.md}.}
\label{tab:exp_seqlen_sweep}
\begin{tabular}{@{}r r r r r@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Frames} & \textbf{Top-1} & \textbf{Top-5} & \textbf{Macro-F1} & \textbf{Balanced Acc.} \\
\midrule
32 & $56.86 \pm 6.12$ & $85.29 \pm 2.95$ & $50.52 \pm 8.27$ & $64.65 \pm 7.48$ \\
64 & $58.82 \pm 2.94$ & $78.43 \pm 4.49$ & $54.88 \pm 0.93$ & $67.42 \pm 0.76$ \\
96 & $54.90 \pm 4.49$ & $81.37 \pm 4.49$ & $49.63 \pm 6.33$ & $61.36 \pm 6.56$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The 64-frame configuration produces the most stable training across the three seeds. Macro-F1 standard deviation drops to $0.93$ pp, and Balanced Accuracy standard deviation to $0.76$ pp, both an order of magnitude tighter than at 32 or 96 frames. The sweep does not select the configuration that maximises any single mean metric, since 32 frames actually edges out 64 on Top-5 (85.29 vs.\ 78.43), but it does select the configuration whose mean is least sensitive to the seed. The selection criterion is reproducibility, not peak Top-1, and that is the criterion this chapter applies to every downstream choice.

\image{results/sequence_length_sweep.png}{0.85}{Per-metric distribution across the sequence-length sweep (3 seeds at 32, 64, and 96 frames). Source: \texttt{05\_experiments/sequence\_length\_sweep/sequence\_length\_comparison.png}}{fig:exp_seqlen}

The 64-frame value is therefore fixed as the default for every downstream experiment in this chapter, including the cross-dataset MS-ASL50 evaluation in Section~\ref{sec:exp_msasl}. A dedicated sweep on MS-ASL50, where median clip length is approximately 80 frames, would be a natural extension and is acknowledged in Section~\ref{sec:exp_limitations}.

\section{WLASL30 Validation}
\label{sec:exp_wlasl30}

Two independent validation protocols characterise the champion model on WLASL30. The first is the \textbf{Stratified 5-Fold Cross-Validation} run on WLASL100 filtered to the same 30 classes (332 total samples; \texttt{05\_experiments/kfold\_wlasl100/kfold\_results.json}), which yields a Top-1 mean of \textbf{63.85\,\% $\pm$ 4.50\,\%} and a Top-5 mean of \textbf{86.46\,\% $\pm$ 2.04\,\%}. This is the headline thesis number because it averages across both initialisation and split, and because the standard deviations are computed across non-overlapping test partitions. The second is the \textbf{multi-seed evaluation} on the fixed WLASL30 split at $\mathit{max\_frames} = 64$ extracted from the sequence-length sweep (Table~\ref{tab:exp_wlasl30_seeds}).

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\caption{Per-seed test results of the GRU+Attention champion on the fixed WLASL30 split (34 test samples). Source: \texttt{05\_experiments/sequence\_length\_sweep/sweep\_summary.csv} (frames=64 rows).}
\label{tab:exp_wlasl30_seeds}
\begin{tabular}{@{}r r r r r r@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Top-1} & \textbf{Top-5} & \textbf{Macro-F1} & \textbf{Balanced Acc.} & \textbf{Best Epoch} \\
\midrule
42   & 55.88 & 73.53 & 55.94 & 68.18 & 40 \\
123  & 61.76 & 79.41 & 54.20 & 66.67 & 29 \\
2026 & 58.82 & 82.35 & 54.49 & 67.42 & 27 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Mean $\pm$ Std} & $58.82 \pm 2.94$ & $78.43 \pm 4.49$ & $54.88 \pm 0.93$ & $67.42 \pm 0.76$ & N/A \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The two protocols disagree by approximately five percentage points on Top-1 (63.85 vs.\ 58.82). The disagreement is informative rather than contradictory: 5-Fold CV varies the test partition itself, so the Top-1 average is taken over five non-overlapping test partitions of approximately 66 samples each ($332 / 5 = 66.4$) and effectively smooths the worst single split; the multi-seed protocol fixes the 34-sample test split and varies only the model initialisation and batch order. Both quantities are real; they answer different questions. The headline number reported elsewhere in the thesis is the 5-Fold mean because it is the more statistically robust of the two and is directly comparable to the K-Fold standard in the SLR literature.

The per-class behaviour of the champion on the fixed split is concentrated on a small number of low-support classes. From \texttt{05\_experiments/final\_assault/per\_class\_metrics.csv}, the worst recall values among supported classes are \emph{fine} (1 sample, recall $0.00$), \emph{thanksgiving} (1, $0.00$), \emph{thin} (2, $0.00$), \emph{candy} (3, $0.33$), \emph{what} (3, $0.33$), and \emph{cool} (2, $0.50$). With one to three test samples per class, even a single misprediction yields a 33--100\,pp recall drop. This is a property of the test split, not of the model: the per-class metric can be recovered only by enlarging the evaluation set, which is exactly what the cross-dataset experiment in Section~\ref{sec:exp_msasl} achieves.

\section{Cross-Dataset Validation: MS-ASL50}
\label{sec:exp_msasl}

The pipeline was tuned on WLASL30. To check whether its behaviour is a property of the method or of the benchmark, the same code path was retrained on MS-ASL50 with no architectural changes and no dataset-specific hyperparameter tuning. The only fields that differ between the two configurations are the dataset path and the gloss vocabulary.

\subsection{Building the MS-ASL50 subset}

MS-ASL ships only annotation JSONs and YouTube URLs; the 25{,}513 declared instances must be retrieved per-clip, and not all of them are still online. The audit at \texttt{06\_datasets/MS-ASL/videos/\_audit.json} records that \textbf{16{,}816 of 25{,}513 clips were successfully downloaded (65.9\,\%)}, with split-level coverage of 74.0\,\% / 47.5\,\% / 58.0\,\% (train / val / test). The drop-out is dominated by YouTube videos that have been removed or made private since the dataset was published.

The subset selection itself is constrained by data deficiency. The disk-level audit at \texttt{06\_datasets/MS-ASL/audit/audit\_report.md} reports that only \textbf{6} of the top-30 classes meet the strict 30 train / 5 val / 5 test threshold; \textbf{17} meet the 20 / 5 / 5 threshold; and \textbf{57} meet the relaxed 20 / 3 / 3 threshold. An MS-ASL30 reproduction is therefore not viable from the locally available data. The chosen alternative is \textbf{MS-ASL50}, defined as the 50 classes with the largest train counts among those satisfying the 20 / 3 / 3 threshold. After landmark extraction (\texttt{06\_datasets/MS-ASL/subsets/msasl50/extraction\_report.md}), 31 source clips are dropped because their mean MediaPipe detection rate falls below the 0.3 floor, leaving \textbf{1{,}410 train / 306 val / 236 test (1{,}952 total)}. The mean detection rate across surviving clips is \textbf{83.9\,\%}, substantially above the WLASL figure (approximately $69\,\%$), consistent with MS-ASL clips being longer and shot under more controlled conditions.

\subsection{Cross-dataset results}

Both datasets were trained with three seeds (42, 123, 2026). Aggregated metrics are in Table~\ref{tab:exp_cross_dataset}, with the MS-ASL50 per-seed breakdown in Table~\ref{tab:exp_msasl_seeds}.

