\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The central observation is that the Top-1 standard deviations on the two datasets are nearly identical (2.94 pp on WLASL30 versus 2.89 pp on MS-ASL50). With 7$\times$ fewer test samples on WLASL30, this is the strongest statement the experiment supports: \emph{training stability is a property of the pipeline, not of a specific benchmark}. The pipeline does not need dataset-specific tuning to produce reproducible results on a second ASL corpus.

The absolute Top-1 figure on MS-ASL50 (70.20) is higher than on WLASL30 (58.82), but this is not evidence that MS-ASL is intrinsically easier. Three structural factors account for the gap. First, MS-ASL50 has approximately 6$\times$ more training samples (1{,}410 vs.\ 242), which is the dominant variable in this data regime. Second, the test set is 7$\times$ larger (236 vs.\ 34), so each individual misclassification moves Top-1 by approximately 0.42 pp instead of 2.94 pp; a finer measurement grid yields more representative values, which usually means closer to the true asymptotic performance. Third, MS-ASL clips are sourced from longer, better-lit YouTube educational material, which is reflected in the higher MediaPipe detection rate. The experiment therefore supports the claim that the pipeline reproduces; it does not support a claim about intrinsic dataset difficulty.

\image{results/confusion_matrix_msasl50_seed2026.png}{0.85}{Row-normalised confusion matrix on the MS-ASL50 test split for seed 2026 (236 samples). The matrices for seeds 42 and 123 are stored alongside in \texttt{05\_experiments/msasl50\_baseline/seed\{42,123\}/}; their qualitative structure is the same. Source: \texttt{05\_experiments/msasl50\_baseline/seed2026/confusion\_matrix\_normalized\_true.png}}{fig:exp_cm_msasl50}

The confusion structure (verified in \texttt{05\_experiments/msasl50\_baseline/seed2026/top\_confusions.json}) is dominated by visually adjacent signs. The largest absolute confusion is \emph{deaf} $\rightarrow$ \emph{need} (4 of 14 samples, 28.6\,\%), followed by \emph{pencil} $\rightarrow$ \emph{write} and \emph{fish} $\rightarrow$ \emph{lost} (each 3 of 5, 60.0\,\%), \emph{nurse} $\rightarrow$ \emph{sit} (3 of 6, 50.0\,\%), and \emph{mother} $\rightarrow$ \emph{woman} (2 of 4, 50.0\,\%). These are recognisable as one-handed, location-similar pairs whose discriminative cue is fine-grained finger shape rather than gross trajectory.

The per-class metrics from \texttt{05\_experiments/msasl50\_baseline/seed2026/per\_class\_metrics.csv} show three classes with $0.00$ recall (\emph{here}, \emph{man}, \emph{woman}, all with three test samples), and six further classes below $50\,\%$ recall (\emph{need} at $0.25$, \emph{school} at $0.29$, \emph{nurse} at $0.33$, \emph{lost} at $0.33$, \emph{write} at $0.40$, \emph{doctor} at $0.43$). At the other end, nineteen classes reach perfect recall on this split, including six with five or more support samples (\emph{teacher} 9, \emph{no} 7, \emph{tired} 7, \emph{deaf} 6, \emph{please} 5, \emph{like} 5). The headline Macro-F1 of 68.08 is therefore an average over a strongly bimodal per-class distribution, which is consistent with the higher Macro-F1 standard deviation (3.44) compared to Top-1 (2.89): per-class recall is a finer-grained signal and amplifies seed-to-seed variation in which classes happen to be learned best.

\section{Vocabulary Scaling}
\label{sec:exp_scaling}

WLASL exposes progressively larger subsets of its 2{,}000-class vocabulary. Retraining the champion configuration on each subset quantifies how the system degrades as the recognition problem grows. The experiments in this section all use a single seed (42); multi-seed validation was not performed beyond 30 classes.

